  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThrough Saturday: A disturbance/MCV lifting northward from the \nGulf Coast will serve as a forcing mechanism for scattered to \nnumerous showers and thunderstorms today, with scattered light \nshowers already ongoing across portions of central and southeast \nMS early this morning. At the same time, an upper shortwave will \nshift from the Ozarks into the Mid South today, providing another \nfocus for development over the northwestern portion of the area. \nThough there will be some morning activity, convective coverage \nwill still primarily be diurnally modulated. Though a few showers \nmay linger into the evening. A belt of marginally stronger upper \nflow will pass across north MS in association with the shortwave \ntoday, and high res guidance does suggest a few strong storms are \npossible in that area, though any threat looks to remain mostly \nnorth of our area. The coastal disturbance will shift east of the \narea tonight. Meanwhile the trailing portion of the Mid South \nupper shortwave will linger into Saturday, meaning scattered \nafternoon and early evening convection is expected again, but \ncoverage should be lesser than today.\n\nWith increasing low level moisture, heat stress will become more \nof a factor over the next few days. For today and Saturday, it \nappears increased clouds/precip will preclude the need for an \nadvisory, though a few spots may still approach or briefly reach \nadvisory thresholds at times. We will maintain a limited heat \nstress threat outlook during this time frame.\n\nSunday through next Friday: Upper ridging will build back in \nacross much of the Southeastern CONUS early next week, resulting \nin a return to higher heat with lower diurnal rain chances more \ntypical of summer. With a muggy airmass still in place, heat \nindices may begin rising to critical thresholds each day. \nInterestingly, the higher heat indices during this period will be \ndriven more by higher temps, as dewpoints mix down into the upper \n60s in many areas during the day. We will highlight an elevated \nheat stress threat for much of the area, and heat advisories may \nultimately be needed. \n\nOver the second half of the week, there are signs the position of\nthe surface ridge will begin to bring some heat stress relief. \nThere is increasing guidance consensus in drier easterly surface \nflow wedging around the Appalachians and toward our area as early \nas Wednesday or Thursday. The eastern half of the area has the \nhighest chance of being a beneficiary of this regime, so it may \nstill be quite hot from the MS River west. We'll keep our fingers \ncrossed for any potential break from this muggy weather. /DL/",
